#!/usr/bin/env python3

"""Privileged Wi-Fi sign-in test for the "wlan-auth" Script v2 sensor.

The sensor script runs as the probe's service user and must not configure
any Wi-Fi. This program takes over exactly that step. The task comes in as
JSON, the result goes out as JSON - credentials thereby never sit in argv
and are not readable via /proc.

It runs as a service that systemd starts on /run/prtg-sensor-wlan-auth.sock
and keeps running, because it schedules the radio itself. A probe can carry
several sensors of this type on the one interface it owns, and PRTG cannot
keep them off each other: it knows an interval per sensor and nothing about
the other sensors on the same device. So the sensors ask for the last
measurement and its age, and this service decides when to measure. See
ADR 0011. Started without a socket from systemd, it stays the filter
program it has always been: JSON on stdin, JSON on stdout, one measurement
on the spot - which is how it is called by hand on the probe.

The route over a socket instead of sudo is necessary because
prtg.mpprobe.service runs with NoNewPrivileges=yes and the kernel then
ignores the setuid bit of sudo. As its own service, this program is also
not subject to the seccomp restrictions of the MPP unit, whose
RestrictAddressFamilies does not allow AF_PACKET - without which there
would be no DHCP request.

It also stays callable directly on the command line: whoever starts it as
root with a JSON task on stdin gets the same answer.

The test runs its own short-lived wpa_supplicant instance on a reserved
test interface. It leaves no network configuration behind: the DHCP stage
runs in test mode, and the interface is powered down again afterwards.
"""

